  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher applied tariffs on US–Canada trade raise costs and customs frictions for cross‑border production and logistics, producing delays and lower volumes of military equipment and dual‑use shipments from North American suppliers destined for Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_canada_tariff_row</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_canada_tariff_row::tariff_rate" target="treasury_market_stress::market_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A rise in applied bilateral tariff rates abruptly changes import prices and corporate profit outlooks and triggers rapid portfolio rebalancing and liquidity shifts, producing larger and more frequent price swings in the treasury market.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_canada_tariff_row</data>
  <data key="d39">treasury_market_stress</data>
</edge>
