<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::chip_manufacturing_cap" target="russia_ukraine_war::critical_supply_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">An increase in advanced chip manufacturing capacity globally reduces scarcity of specialized components used in drones, guidance and comms, lowering the exposure of conflict-relevant supply chains to interruption or rerouting.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::chip_manufacturing_cap" target="treasury_market_stress::long_term_yields" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Policies and large public subsidies to expand domestic advanced chip capacity (driven by US–China rivalry) raise expected government outlays and future debt supply, pressuring long-term yields higher as markets price increased fiscal financing needs.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">treasury_market_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::chip_manufacturing_cap" target="us_canada_tariff_row::tariff_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">A rapid increase in US domestic advanced chip capacity is typically accompanied by industrial-policy measures (local-content rules, procurement preferences and safeguard tariffs) that raise applied import tariffs on competing semiconductor inputs and finished goods from trading partners, including Canada.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d39">us_canada_tariff_row</data>
</edge>
